Draft:
During the incident response phase of an attack that spanned the year 2020, our team identified multiple advanced persistent threat (APT) actors, including both nation-state and criminal groups, as the perpetrators. The attack exploited a range of known vulnerabilities, including CVE-2017-11882, CVE-2019-19781, CVE-2019-11510, CVE-2018-13379, CVE-2020-5902, CVE-2020-15505, CVE-2020-0688, CVE-2019-3396, CVE-2017-11882, CVE-2019-11580, CVE-2018-7600, CVE-2019-18935, CVE-2019-0604, CVE-2020-0787, and CVE-2020-1472. These vulnerabilities were leveraged to gain unauthorized Remote Code Execution (RCE) on target systems. The primary target was the Pulse Connect Secure (PCS) Virtual Private Network (VPN) servers, which are commonly used by public and private organizations, particularly those involved in the COVID-19 vaccine development. The attackers were able to steal the unencrypted credentials of all users on a compromised PCS server, enabling them to maintain unauthorized access even after the system was patched. This persistence was achieved because the attackers had the capability to retain access until all compromised credentials were changed. The impact of the attack was significant, as it compromised sensitive data and allowed unauthorized access to critical systems, posing a serious threat to the security and confidentiality of the organizations' operations.

